5 Mistakes That Cost You Marks Every Single Time
These aren't random errors. These are the exact patterns that repeat in SSC CGL and Bank exams year after year. Examiners know students fall for them. They keep using them because they work.

Mistake 1: Treating Negative Words as Positive

Words like hardly, scarcely, barely, never, no, none, nobody, nothing carry a negative meaning even without "not." A sentence with these words is already negative, so it needs a positive tag. Students see no "not" and automatically write a negative tag. The examiner smiles.

Mistake 2: Using "Amn't I?" for "I am" Statements

When the statement is "I am..." the correct negative tag is "aren't I?" — not "amn't I" and not "isn't I." This is a non-negotiable rule that appears in almost every SSC paper.

Mistake 3: Confusing "Let us" with "Let me"

"Let us go" takes "shall we?" because it's a suggestion including the speaker. "Let me go" takes "will you?" because it's a request to someone else. Students mix these up constantly. The exam banks on it.

Mistake 4: Forgetting the Pronoun Switch for Indefinite Pronouns

Everyone, somebody, anybody, nobody, no one — all of these take "they" in the question tag, not "he" or "she." This is one of the most repeated traps in Bank PO papers.

