The 7-Step Rule System (With Real Exam Questions)
Now let's build your system. These 7 steps cover every question tag pattern that has appeared in SSC CGL, IBPS PO, and Railways exams from 2021 to 2025. Each step has a real exam question — not a made-up example.

Step 1: The Fundamental Rule — Positive vs. Negative

Positive statement → Negative tag. Negative statement → Positive tag. Sounds obvious. But "negative" includes hidden negatives.

SSC CHSL (10+2) DEO & LDC Exam Question:

"She pretends as if she has never in her life told a lie. Isn't it?"

The tag "Isn't it?" is wrong. The statement contains "never" (hidden negative), so the tag should be positive: "Has she?"

Step 2: Match the Auxiliary Verb Exactly

Whatever auxiliary (helping verb) appears in the statement must appear in the tag. No substitutes.

SSC CGL 2022 Tier-I Question:

"You fell on your back, ________?"

The verb "fell" is past tense (V2). No auxiliary in the statement. So we use "didn't you?" The helping verb "did" matches the simple past tense.

Step 3: The Subject Must Become a Pronoun

Never use a noun in the tag. Always convert the subject to its corresponding pronoun.

Bank PO Pattern Question (Repeated 2021–2024):

"Anjali dances very well, ________?"

"Anjali" becomes "she." The statement is positive, so the tag is negative. Answer: "doesn't she?"

Step 4: The "I am" Exception

This is the most tested exception in SSC exams. When the statement uses "I am," the negative tag is "aren't I?"

SSC CGL 2023 Tier-I Question Pattern:

"I am looking after the garden, ________?"

Answer: "aren't I?" — not "amn't I" and not "isn't I." This rule never changes.

Step 5: Indefinite Pronouns Always Take "They"

Everyone, somebody, anybody, nobody, no one, everyone — all become "they" in the tag.

IBPS PO 2022 Prelims Question Pattern:

"Everyone wanted a ticket, ________?"

Answer: "didn't they?" — not "didn't he" and not "didn't she."

Step 6: Imperatives and "Let us" Rules

Positive imperatives → "will you?" or "won't you?" (both accepted in modern English). Negative imperatives → "will you?" only. "Let us" → "shall we?"

SSC CGL 2021 Tier-I Question:

"Do as I say, ________?"

Answer: "won't you?" — this is an imperative (command), so "will you?" or "won't you?" work. In SSC, "won't you?" is the preferred answer.

Step 7: "There" as Subject Stays "There"

When "there" is the subject of the statement, it remains "there" in the tag. No pronoun substitution needed.

Bank Exam Pattern Question (2021–2025):

"There is no milk in the fridge, ________?"

Answer: "is there?" — "there" stays as the subject. The statement is negative ("no milk"), so the tag is positive.

